After a little experimentation, I found a way to boot my XL 300 from the
Red Hat 5.0 CD-ROM.  This system uses AlphaBIOS; I don't have access
to a system with ARC console firmware, but I suspect a similar method
would work.

I created a boot selection like this:

Boot File:  CD:                   \milo\arc\linload.exe
OS Path:    CD:                   \milo\xlt-alco
OS Options: boot sr0:/kernels/xlt.gz root=/dev/fd0 load_ramdisk=1

Note the "OS Path".  The name of the MILO image must be truncated to
eight characters, as AlphaBIOS is limited to the 8.3 filename convention.
You must of course substitute the (truncated) name of the MILO image
and path to the kernel for the type of system you're booting.  Note also
that AlphaBIOS will complain about this boot selection when you try to
save it, even though it will work.

Note also that a root floppy is still required, but it is platform-
independent.  Perhaps Red Hat could take note of this technique in the
manual, and even rearrange the CD-ROM to eliminate the root floppy?

-- 
Bill Roman  (roman@songdog.eskimo.com / roman@songdog.uucp)   running linux


