Not to bring up an old discussion, but, a programmer tends to ask
around about what other people are experiencing with the same SW
before deciding to bother the maintainer of the SW. The maintainer
may decide that it's a bug report when a question about observed
bad behavior is discussed but it isn't necessarily so. I
prefer to confirm a problem among 'birds of a feather' before
bringing the problem to the attention of the person who claims
responsibility for the SW. For example, if I happened to also
see SIG 6 in my work, I'd probably chime in to the poster and
encourage them to bring the bug forward or do it myself. Since
I haven't had that problem, I have no comments about the symptoms.

However, I too have noticed performance degrade from the original
promising results back in November. That has been offset by
bug fixes so it's a trade off about whether to bother anyone
about it. For now, I'll wait on the next release and see how it
works when it becomes available. I assume the maintainers know
performance is important and it will improve over time. There
are a couple standard ways to test it so my telling them my
code isn't performing fast enough is not going to help.

My $.02.


Wes


