Spec95 is good. Also run Linpack in FORTRAN and C.
I've found Linpack is quite good at predicting how well
tuned code will run. Compare the Alpha result to
DEC UNIX, SGI IRIX, and RS/6000 AIX. Those systems have
good compilers/performance.

To give you an idea what I'm doing, my tests use the following:

Input dataset: 2.6 GB per run
Working dataset: 19 GB split among N processors

Program runs on N processors. Currently I use 2 or 4 for tests.
Target system is 32 processors. A reasonable run takes 4
processors around 20000 seconds. Isolated tests are difficult
due to the way files effect performance. Also, the dataset
used to generate the working dataset can significantly impact
the main runs. My current algorithm shows processors 95-98% 
busy in user space for the above test. The working dataset
can potentially have 80+GB read from it per node.(It's not, I
do some sorting to prevent that) Where once my Alphas
were about 80% of my RS/6000's, they are now no more than
50%.

I have other simple tests that show little performance 
change so I can't put my hands on a small test case that shows
what's wrong. However the tests do show where G77/Alpha is 
relative to a good FORTRAN compiler.

If you can make Linpack 100x100 run fast, both double precision
and single precision, I would wager you fix my problem at the 
same time.

My simple benchmark measures individual operations in
FORTRAN, like add/sub/mult/div/sum, for real*4, real*8, and
complex. My RS/6000 (397) runs significantly faster than my
PC164LX 533 Alpha on those. I can give you the source and my
RS/6000 results off line if you're interested. I had
someone run the tests on DEC FORTRAN too and from what I remember,
it was similar to the RS/6000. Also have access to a 195MHZ
SGI R10000 and can run tests on it for your comparison if
interested. For a reference, on my last test, an RS/6000
sustains 618 MFLOPS out of a theoretical max of 640. The
PC164LX with G77 does 302 out of 1066.

 
Wes


