David Alan Gilbert <dg@cogency.co.uk> wrote:
... (replying to Richard Henderson) ... 
> The reason I haven't reported the X problem to the EGCS list is that I'm
> not sure whether its an X problem or an EGCS problem; and I don't know
> enough about the internals of X/VGA text modes/The Matrox millenium etc to
> debug it.

	I know several UGLY things inside very recent Matrox drivers.
	("very recent" is likely the lattest public source.  I haven't
	 had time to check developements during past two months.)
	Very likely those problems are still there..

	Basically there is code with does something like this:

		char *crtbuf = ... ;
		char *aligned_buf = (char *)(((int) crtbuf) & ~3);

	This works just fine on 32-bit systems, but at Alphas...

	Then there is another case where the system expects to have
	32-bit longs while it is scanning in a loop and storing something
	through:    *longptr++ = ... ;
	(Naturally the object is not aligned properly at the start
	 of the loop for this type of scan..)

	The first error blew up my X-server every time I did something
	in NetScape (displayed at Alpha, but running elsewere), and
	I finally fixed it.  The second is "just" spewing my dmesg with
	"unaligned trap" messages.

	I did report that first case to XFree86-bugs, but heard nothing
	back from them.

