Subject: Fun with assembler
In-Reply-To: <Pine.LNX.3.95.980602112546.480A-100000@riha.physik.Uni-Osnabrueck.DE>
X-Status: N
X-Mailer: Applixware 4.3 (807.65.0.22)

Finally getting somewhere with this alpha.

First some history.

We are working on some 64 bit code which isn't being optimised very well with 
gcc/egcs and as a result our 533Mhz alpha was being trounced by the MIPS 
R10000s in our SGI Challenge which only run at 200Mhz.  Said trouncing was of 
the order of 15% poorer performance for the alpha.  Grim situation for us, big 
laughs from the SGI boys.

Needless to say the problem is with the compilers.  The one on the SGI is smart 
enough to know how to get the best out of the chip, gcc/egcs is not.  Of course 
there is the option of DU C which is supposed to give about 30% better results 
than gcc.  I can now believe this having improved the performance of our code 
by a similar amount by a few simple changes to the assembler output of egcs.

The problem is that the alpha quite often gets stalled if you write code which 
is very tight, ie plenty of registers used and each instruction relies on the 
previous one.  The trick to getting better performance is to try and pair up 
your instructions but you can only really do this at the assembly level.  The 
good news is it is easy, you just need to find the busiest part of your 
program, in our case its an intensive repetitive operation in a loop.  Going 
through this code it quickly became apparent that the alpha was being stalled 
like mad, a typical example being:

   srl $3,7,$1
   srl $4,7,$2

The srl instruction must go down pipeline E0 and because there are two of these 
you end up with pipe E1 doing nothing.  The solution, stick an instruction in 
between the two:

   srl $3,7,$1
   addq $8,$6,$8
   srl $4,7,$2

Now the addq is using the second pipeline.  Do the same for the second srl and 
you're off and running.

Most instructions can run in both E0 and E1 but some are E0 or E1 only.  Mix 
and match, don't be affraid to put nop instructions in to pair up with an srl 
or whatever that you can't pair up with something else, you must try and keep 
things in step.

I have put the pdf files I got this from on my machine for anonymous ftp, you 
can get it from nova.bru.ed.ac.uk in the /pub directory, the original site for 
these is
 http://www.partner.digital.com/www-swdev/pages/Home/TECH/documents/alpha_cookboo
k/biblio.htm.  

The most interesting section is on the internal architecture.  There are good 
descriptions and examples of code to look at.  Now, if I can just squeeze a bit 
more out of it, we're already 30% faster than the MIPS, there must be something 
more I can do...... :-)

Dr. Shane Sturrock - sss@holyrood.ed.ac.uk
