Quoting Michal Jaegermann (michal@ellpspace.math.ualberta.ca):
> Paul Tomblin writes:
> > 
> > When I reported here that when I compiled my kernel with egcs that X would no
> > longer start, all I got was derision and "helpful" suggestions that I did
> > something wrong.  Well, guess what - it's now official that egcs optimizes out
> > an assignment that is necessary to make X work.
> 
> The file in question, which "officially" creates problems for X when
> not fixed (it is modified in development kernels), is
>   arch/i386/kernel/ioport.c
> so, unless you are running Intel kernel on your box, I fail to see how
> this may affect your Alpha.

Well, I compiled my kernel using egcs (using a make clean; make dep; make
boot; make modules; make modules_install, so there was NO CHANGE IN
CONFIGURATION), and X wouldn't start.  I switched back to gcc, and compiled
using the same sequence of commands and it would.  So they *know* about the
ioport.c problem, but there is something similar in the alpha kernel (at least
in 2.0.30).  I'll give it another try with egcs 1.0.3a and kernel 2.0.34, and
let you all know.

Does anybody know if the alpha patches that were applied to 2.0.30 by RedHat
were incorportated in the basic 2.0.34 kernel?  I'd like to get it before
RedHat gets around to supplying it.

BTW: I agree with you that comparing the results with a broken program like
qtscape is probably not valid - if it's broken one way with gcc, then that
same breakage can show up in a different way with a different compiler.

