Ken,
	You have several options - the basic questons are as follows:

Older hardware vs. newest - If I had it all to do over again, I might
just get an SX building block from http://www.dcg.com/ for about $900,
but the older machines are pretty affordable. You can get 166 Mhz
motherboards (with no cache) for about $100 - that will give you about
P90 speed with built-in SCSI and IDE - this is the popular NONAME or
AXPci33 motherboard (available from many places, including BNFE in NH
http://www.bnfe.com ).

Stepping up the speed ladder there were 233 Mhz versions for a bit
more money (I got mine for $200 from HardData in Canada) which has
better performance, but it willnot "blow away" a P133 in most cases.

Both of the above systems need hard drives, CD-ROM, floppy, a video
card and lots of memory (and probably a case/power supply).

There are also Multia/UDBs with essentially the same CPUs as the above
but with built-in networking, video, sound, floppy and HD in a small
case - they are nice and cheap, with many sources on the net (anyone
else got some pointers on this?). A Multia/UDB is a good "getting
started in Alpha" machine, but you may want more speed very soon.

Then there are the various DEC systems (Alpha stations/servers, etc.)
which I am unfamiliar with, but have a broad range of included options
and prices (but more than the Multia/UDBs). I got an Alphastation 200
4/166 recently at http://www.onsale.com/ for $320 with a graphics
adapter and 24 Meg RAM as well as the built-in floppy, sound, SCSI,
and ethernet, a pretty good deal (others paid less, some even paid
more!) to get started with - I only need to add an HD and I'm ready
to go...

THe building blocks from http://www.dcg.com/ are very interesting to
me, and if I was just starting out I would get one of those (probably
the SX version, slower speed but also has can use cheaper non-parity
memory) - these are Alpha motherboards in a case with a power suppply
priced at under $1000 ($895 last time I looked at the site for SX)
and at 533 Mhz and with a 1 Meg cache, pretty speedy machines!

There is an LX version that has better performance (partly because of
the 2 Meg cache)that is a bit more ($1300 or so?) and requires that
the memory be true parity.

Neither the SX or the LX have built-in SCSI, sound, video or networking,
so those are all expenses you will have to consider.

Having said all that, if you want to go low, try a Multia/UDB or a
noname machine that is already built-up*. If you can spend a few more
dollars try for one of the DEC Aplha system (pre-configured & complete).
If money is no object, and you just wnat a REALLY FAST ALpha, I would
try and justify one of the SX- or LX-based building blocks.

* I am considering selling my noname as part of my preparing for an
SX building block, if you would be interested, please email me directly,
I can sell it complete and ready to run or as stripped as you would
like.

Good luck, I hope the above helps...

Ken
khansen@njcc.com

