>Needless to say the problem is with the compilers.  The one on the SGI is
>smart enough to know how to get the best out of the chip, gcc/egcs is not. 
>Of course there is the option of DU C which is supposed to give about
>30% better results than gcc.  I can now believe this having improved the
>performance of our code by a similar amount by a few simple changes to the
>assembler output of egcs.

I've been doing similar benchmarks at NASA Ames with a computational
fluid dynamics (CFD) code along with a trajectory code that I wrote.  The
CFD code is a commercial FORTRAN/C hybrid code. My code is written purely in
C.  Initially I could only benchmark "toy problems" for the CFD code using an
Egcs compiled executable because the code would crash if I ran a real
test case.  However the latest version of Egcs will run real test cases but
I haven't benchmarked it.  I did benchmark the Egcs compiled toy CFD problems
and found that the SGI/R10000 was significantly faster than my 533 Mhz Alpha. 
However I found for my pure C language trajectory code the Alpha was faster. 
Keep in mind that this is an apples-to-oranges comparison because the
trajectory code was scalar while the CFD code was vector intensive.  Also the
Gcc compiler is a much more mature compiler than then the G77 compiler.  I
then compiled the CFD code as a staticly linked executable using DEC Unix
Fortran and C compilers.   The DEC compiled code ran without mishap for a
full blown test case (3D simulation of the Venture Star RLV) under Linux and
was about 15% ***faster*** than our fastest SGI/R10000.  My Alpha with 6
Gbytes of hard disk space, 1/4 Gbyte of RAM and a 20" moniter costs about
$6,500 all up. My Alpha ran faster for our toughest CFD problem against an
SGI box that cost $250,000.  In terms of CPU cycles/dollar the Linux/Alpha
combination "blows the doors off" of all competitors.  It is without
question the cheapest way to do scientific compuations.


                                     Gary


