In patch-2.5 function 're_input()' in inp.c may attempt to free
unallocated i_buffer and i_ptr resulting in a possible "Segmentation
violation".  This happens, for example, on Linux/Alpha with 
Red Hat distributed binaries patch-2.5-2.alpha.rpm.

An illegal free may occur when, in a chain of patches, patch attempts
to create a new file, which already exists, and this attempt is denied.
A call to 'reinitialize_almost_everything()', which follows, invokes
're_input()' and a disaster may happen if 'using_plan_a' was already
true.

The following simple fix prevents this:

--- patch-2.5/inp.c~	Mon Jul 21 11:59:46 1997
+++ patch-2.5/inp.c	Thu Jun  4 20:13:44 1998
@@ -58,8 +58,14 @@
 re_input()
 {
     if (using_plan_a) {
-	free (i_buffer);
-	free (i_ptr);
+	if (0 != i_buffer) {
+	    free (i_buffer);
+	    i_buffer = 0;
+	}
+	if (0 != i_ptr) {
+	    free (i_ptr);
+	    i_ptr = 0;
+	}
     }
     else {
 	close (tifd);

