I'd found similar problems when cross building Linux/ARM kernels - we hit
a bug which only occurred if we built using EGCS and we of course blamed the
compiler; in the end we came to the conclusion that EGCS was making an
optimisation that the earlier compilers hadn't and it had broken because
the kernel source made presumptions which were rather dodgy.
So don't always blame the compiler.
But this is a problem - when we find somethign which runs differently
if compiled with EGCS I only like to report it to the EGCS list
if I'm reasonably sure that its the compiler thats broken.  With
big programs which I dont know my way around the source code of (like X)
I'm never sure if its EGCS or the code being compiled so am never
sure how to report the bug.

Dave
