On Fri, 5 Jun 1998, Frank Louwers wrote:

> - The sound Card is M$ compatible, any problems to expect?

Works OK, under 2.0.30 at leats. Under 2.0.33 you'll need too modify
something in the code, but that's easy (see the archive of this list)

> - The network card, is it supported? Which one is it?

It's a builtin NIC, a DEC DE434/5 and is of course supported.

> - Am i correct that the unit has 2 PCMCIA cards? Do dey work under Linux?

Yes, there are 2 PC-card (AKA PCMCIA) slots. I've heard they work, but
never used them myself

> - How does the boot-up work (There is no lilo, but milo? what  's the
> differece?)

Booting an Alpha is something you'll need to get used to. In the UDB's
Firmware (you might compare this with an intel's Bios/CMOS) there are two
bootloaders available, known as ARC and SRM, one intended for booting NT
and the other for booting Digital Unix, but both are able to load the so
called MILO, and that is just some code that is able to unzip and boot
your kernel.

> - How does the installation work? The unit has no CDROM, can I install it
> NFS?

That's how I got mine up, using NFS (or FTP, I don't know, too long ago).

> - The video card, is it supported by X? (xfree86?)

Yes, but it can only do 8bit color (so only 256 colors).

> - someone told me programs for RISC processors are bigger than those for
> CISC, so 340 MB won't be enough to install a decent Linux ... is 500 MB
> enough?

RISC binaries are larger, so some extra money spent on memory and maybe
disks might be needed. I'm using this UDB I have with only the 340MB disk
and all homedirs and datafiles are NFS-mounted, and it just fits.

> - speaking of processors, i heard there is a linux x86 emulator. Does that
> mean I can run i386 Linux programs on it?

Yes, but don't excpect to get a very fast emulation, those UDB's are not
very fast (compare a 166 MHz UDB with a 120MHz pentium or so).

> 
> PS: this maillinglist being organised by redhat, why should I use RedHat
> for Alpha in stead of Debian for Alpha? (i know, it is still in
> development, but that shouldn't take long anymore)

I don't know how stable Debian/Alpha is, but for the Alpha RedHat might be
a better choise, because it's currently better supported. For intel's I
use debian only...

Tot ziens,

