A base UDB w/Hard Drive and I assume no RAM - for $100 is a good deal...

For comparison, to build an Alpha system from pieces you would spend:

	Network Card		$20
	Video Card		$20
	Sound			$10
	SCSI			$40
	small drive		$25

Add to the above PCMCIA (which, by the way, is organized as two side-by-
side slots, so you can only use Type II cards, not the thicker Type
III),
and the processor and at $100 you are coming out way ahead for a
low-cost
intro to Alpha.

Speed is not the best, but it will be much better than the 486/DX33 and
way less than the 233 Mhz Pentium.

There is one caveat - the prices above are ESTIMATES for reasonable,
Alpha Linux parts, and if you were to upgrade later, the components
listed above are on the motherboard, so you cannot move them easily.

If you plan on being involved in Alphas for a while, you should probably
invest in a machine that is more upgradeable, but to be honest, if I
had the chance to buy a 166 Mhz UDB for $100, I would seriously think
about it - they are fun little boxes and always make good X Terminals
if nothing else (BTW, the TGA is supported pretty well under WinNT, but
you would REALLY want to upgrade your HD).

If the system includes any RAM, take it! Parity RAM sells for $25/16 Meg
SIMM...

Good luck, and if your source has quantity, please let me know how to
get
in touch...

