With regards to your message at 09:02 AM 06-05-98 +0100,
james@noether.ex.ac.uk. Where you stated:
>>then compiled the CFD code as a staticly linked executable using DEC Unix
>>Fortran and C compilers.   The DEC compiled code ran without mishap for a
>>full blown test case (3D simulation of the Venture Star RLV) under Linux and
>>was about 15% ***faster*** than our fastest SGI/R10000.
>
>Ive seen a couple of people mention using dec fortran under linux.
>Would someone like to give me a few pointers on how one would go about
>this?
Certainly, However, it is not difficult, except in the case of legalities.
One must own a copy of Digital UNIX for the machine, as you will have to
use some of the libraries. Of course you must also own a copy of Digital
Fortran too..
  As these are quite costly, this is often an impediment to many.

You can compile the code on Fortran on another box running UNIX and
Fortran, but the first limitation applies. From what has been posted
earlier by some representatives from Digital, even running statically
linked UNIX applications on Linux may still constitute a copyright violation.



