> It doesn't work for me.  Under 2.0.30, I get:

> Warning: 
>     Name: bookmarkList
>     Class: XmLGrid
>     Attempt to destroy dropSite info for widget that
> hasn't been added as a dropSite.

I got the same with the Motif frontend.
Its very buggy concerning 64 platforms.

So I decided to try the QT frontend and that works for me, at least
the program comes up and you can view simple pages (including frames).
As someone else reported earlier on, this frontend is far from beeing
complete and the mozilla core itself is still very buggy :(

At least the Java-Script part needs some hackery to get things going.
There is a config headerfile where they assume an Intel platform if
LINUX is defined, leading to the wrong size and alignment defines
for the whole Java stuff:

In mozilla/js/ref/prcpucfg.h
.
.
#ifdef XP_UNIX
#if defined(AIXV3)
.
.
#elif defined(LINUX)
.
.
#define PR_BYTES_PER_LONG      4L
.
.
wheras the correct defines can be found a little later on in this file:
.
.
#elif defined(OSF1)
.
.

So this is an example for the "Linux-is-the-Intel-PC-UNIX" mentality.
There are many other parts that would need some care, but as a lack
of time and motivation I left them alone so far.

I wonder how they ever managed to compile a Digital-UNIX version.
Must have been some other sources or the right things happen if
OSF1 is defined instead of LINUX (as they know that OSF is a 64-bit
system, whereas they think of Linux as a 32-bit PC operating system)

Maybe we should try to compile with OSF1 set and LINUX unset?
At least someone should walk through the code and examine every
occurence of LINUX and if there is an equivalent OSF1 definition
that should be used instead.

