>>> "Robert M. Toups, Jr." said:
> 
> Is there anyway
> to get this sucker to boot without the help of a second party machine?

Certainly, from either SRM or ARC consoles.

But I'm confused; in your initial posting, you said:

	I made boot floppies for Milo and Ram Disk. I have gone
	through an entire FTP RedHat install, formatting every
	partition. RedHat seemed to have no troubles installing
	over the T1.

and then:

	When the RedHat install finishes and reboots, I am returned
	to the SRM.

Did you really make *and* use a MILO floppy? That's only useful from ARC
console, when you indicated that your machine was using SRM. From SRM, you
would be using only a kernel floppy, made from avanti-s.img, right?

Anyway, I'll assume you are using SRM console; here's some pointers for
setting up SRM booting:

1. you MUST create a BSD-style partition table on the boot disk; this can be
   done using fdisk's "b" option. SRM does not understand DOS-style partition
   tables, which is the default type that fdisk (or Disk Druid) creates.

2. you MUST leave 1024 sectors in front of the first partition, for space into
   which the bootstrapper (aboot) will be installed. This is critical.

3. use "swriteboot" (info in those webpages) to actually do the install of
   aboot to the harddisk.

If you did NOT either create the BSD-style partition table or leave enough
space, I'm afraid you'll have to install over again, after rectifying the
situation, unless you want to continue booting from floppy.

And normally, you boot one more time from floppy after the install, at which
time you run "swriteboot" to install the bootstrapper, and from then on can
boot directly off the harddisk, via something like:

	>>> boot dka0 -file 2/vmlinux.gz -flags "root=/dev/sda2"

assuming that partition 2 is both root and where vmlinux.gz is found.

I like to partition for SRM booting with partition 1 being swap, and 2 being
root, then you don't have to tell the kernel "root=/dev/sda2" since that is
its default... :-)

And note that you can set-up the SRM envvars "boot_file" to "2/vmlinux.gz"
and "bootdef_dev" to "dka0" and "boot_osflags" to "root=/dev/sda2" (or whatever
is appropriate for your configuration), and then simply type "boot" at the
SRM prompt.

> Or can I use the 4/233 OSF/1 to assist in the install on the 4/166?

It *can* help do some things (use "dd" to make the floppies, serve the CD
over NFS, etc).

> Can I compile a Kernel on a non-Linux box?

It used to be possible, but not many did it, so I don't know if it really
is possible any more. Better to use your freshly installed box... :-)

Good luck.

--Jay++

