IMHO, Digital UNIX is the millstone that has destroyed DEC's
marketing efforts.  They've put a lot of work into it, and have
tuned it over the years. But somehow they managed to alienate
both the BSD and SYSV zealots. :-( They continue to ask us to pay
for something we really don't want.  The user base is just too
small and their NT strategy can only hurt it more.  I don't think
licensing it for Merced will be the answer.

With Samsung producing the KP21264 and KP21264A, the
price/performance of Alpha systems is going to be very competitive,
across the spectrum.  The uni-, dual-, and quad-motherboards with
fast interconnects will make the systems better balanced than
before.

Has DEC woken up to the fact that Beowulf can sell a whole lot of
Alpha hardware?  If Alpha motherboards are price competitive with
high-end Intel systems (and they are / will be), there are lots
of reasons to go with Alpha: better fp/cpu, higher bandwidth,
etc.  But nobody is going to pay the per-cpu license fees for
Digital Unix when Linux/Alpha or NetBSD gets the job done.  Judging
by the rate at which people were snapping up Extreme Linux CDs at Linux
Expo, I'd say there is plenty of interest in personal and
departmental supercomputing.

DEC needs to figure out how it can profit most from their
motherbards and development tools, not their OS.  They've lost
that battle.  I'm willing to pay for quality development tools
that squeeze performance out of my system.  I'm not willing pay
for OS licenses, when I don't require any of the heavyweight
functionality that the OS provides.  Sure, Linux lacks NUMA, etc.
But given the installed base, there is little chance that
Digital UNIX can keep pace with Linux in the areas that anyone
cares about without continuing to be an enormous cash drain.

I'm not saying that DEC should ditch Digital Unix; it is still *the*
robust commercial solution on Alpha; it *is* where people run
Oracle, etc.  But this may not be the case in a few years.  What
I am saying is that they should not let their concerns about
"cannibalizing" Digital Unix sales drive their whole UNIX
marketing strategy.  The market for Digital UNIX is for the moment
distinct from that for Linux and NT; in the future, the latter two
will far surpass the former, provided they take the right approach.

I use both Solaris and Linux on Intel at work.  Read the press
releases:  Linux versions of software are usually available long
before Solaris x86 versions.  NASA chose Solaris x86 for laptops
for the space shuttle; weeks later Sun gave up on supporting
laptop peripherals.  If Alpha becomes a commodity competitor to
Intel, software will appear on NT and Linux; don't hold your
breath for the Digital UNIX versions.


  Bill Rugolsky


