I agree DEC are shooting themselves in the foot. Seems like you have
competing groups within the company. The OS and compiler people
and of course, the HW division. The SW folks probably have a 'quota'
that says they will sell  $xxxxx of SW. Sometimes it's hard for a big
company to see past their divisions bickering and understand that they
are synergistic. One builds on the other's work and in the end, providing
a reasonably priced OS/compiler will drive HW sales. I only started
looking into Alphas when I found Linux was running on them. I'd be
more than willing to pay the price of DEC's FORTRAN if it were
equal to the selling price under NT. I don't currently see NT as a usable
replacement for UNIX boxes so for now I live with Linux and gcc/g77.
With the prices Maurice quoted, I can buy another PC164LX box,
which in this framework would be my decision since my benchmark
showed for reasonably complex loops, g77 is about 70+% of DEC's
FORTRAN. Simple loops are only about 33% though... I try to
avoid those. Unrolling helps a little but not much last I tried.


Wes


