From: "Scott Spieker" <scspieker@ncweb.com> Sat, 6 Jun 1998

> Hi,
> I recently got my hands on a UDB system as well as a serial terminal.  I
> would like very much to get the terminal to work on the serial port of the
> system.  I am using Red Hat 5.0 and I have a null model connection to the

You may find that the UDB serial port is not wired the same as the HP,
especially the control signals - it took me a bit of effort to get the
wiring right for my terminal. If you're getting screen output, though,
it seems that the wiring should be okay.

> Then, later in the boot process the following error message pops up:
> 
> bios32.sio_fixup: weird, device 8086:0484 coming in on slot 7 with no irq
> line!

I may be wrong, but I think this is the PCI-ISA bridge, which doesn't
identify itself properly as a bridge chip, so the kernel thinks it's
an I/O chip which should have an IRQ. I don't think this is a problem.

You may already know this, but this is kinda my checklist:
1. The kernel has an option to send all messages to the first serial
   port. If you want these messages, you have to make sure it's enabled.
2. Logins on the serial ports are not automatically enabled. You have
   to enable them in "/etc/inittab" (and possibly fix the getty setup
   files).
3. If the terminal is your only access device, try setting the console
   device to point to the serial line. You may need this to run in
   single-user mode. You also have to list the serial port as a
   "trusted" device so you can login as root.

Some of the above changes (enabling the serial port) can only be done
after you login as root (chicken and egg? thing). I had to buy a
monitor switch so I could use a monitor on my UDB and other computer
to get things set up to use the terminal. You might have to "borrow"
a monitor to do the setup - after that you should be able to use just
the terminal.

Hope this helps,
I'm not an expert at this stuff, but feel free to email me if you
need more details.
Larry Z.



