I found that in the last couple months my floppy drive on my DEC Multia
has died.  I get bad reads (but not writes) on certain tracks.  This
seemed like a big deal because I wanted to upgrade RedHat 5.0 to 5.1.  I
checked out the hadware connections cleaned it with cleaning disks, but no
luck.

This is how I managed to get it install anyway, even without a floppy
drive.

Requirements:
1. You need two spare partitions that you can crap up.  These must be
larger
   or the same size as the disk images.  I used the DOS partion and swap 
   partition on my internal drive, since I now use a 4GIG external
exclusively.  
2. You need a way of getting the RH5.1 disk images to your HD without
   floppy.  (net or transplant HDs etc)
3. Having linload.exe and milo on a DOS partition.

Procedure:
1. Use fdisk to make sure the partitions are tagged as ext2.
2. dd if=noname.img of=/dev/sda1
3. dd if=ramdisk.img | gunzip > /dev/sda2

Now at the milo prompt instead of using:
	boot fd0:vmlinux.gz root=/dev/fd0 prompt_ramdisk=1 load_ramdisk=1
You can do this instead:
	boot sda1:vmlinux.gz root=/dev/sda2

Cool huh?  It was for me, cuz I didn't know if a regular LAPTOP floppy
drive would work in my Multia/UDB.

I thought a while about this.  I believe you can even setup the partitions
from an Intel Linux machine onto an external SCSI drive, then transplant
this HD to your Alpha.  Then point the arc to the right places...  Easy
huh?

Oh one last note...  You gotta get this right on the first time, because
booting off these partitions marks them as dirty.  So you have to go fsck
them again.  (I had some problems on a second install (after I thought my
"upgrade" was crapped up where by running fdisk in the setup, setup needed
to reboot.)  You might be able to pass "ro" on the boot line, to avoid
this, I'm not sure.

Hope this helps someone out there.

-Scott Serr


