On Fri, 5 Jun 1998, Robert M. Toups, Jr. wrote:

> My problem is that the formated drive will not boot Linux. I am assuming
> that the Seagate Drive is not suited for this model AlphaStation. But then
> again I hate assuming things.  When the RedHat install finishes and
> reboots, I am returned to the SRM. At this point, I believe I am supposed
> to submit the command "boot dka0" to jump into the Linux Drive. When I do
> this, SRM reports back the Block 0 is not a valid boot block and there is a
> bootstrap error.
> 
> I guess my main questions are:
> 
> 1) Are there certain SCSI drives I should use with it? If so, what models?
> 2) Am I using SRM properly to boot the Linux Drive?
> 3) Was spending $261 on an AlphaStation 200 4/166 with 24megs of RAM a good
> deal?

I had to use fdisk with the bsd style disk labelling (b- submenu) and set
aside a 2MB partition as boot.
After installation , ( please make sure that aboot is installed.)
/sbin/swriteboot -f1 -v /dev/sda /boot/bootlx will write the boot loader
to the initial sectors that will make dka0 bootable.

SRM-Howto will give more details.
Regards
