>>> Stefan van der Eijk said:
> 
> I'm having some problems installing
> RedHat Linux onto my Digital Personal Workstation
> (alpha) which contains a Qlogic ISP1040 SCSI
> controller. The SCSI controller is recognized as a
> ISP1020, but I can't install the OS. for more
> details on what goes wrong see below.
> 
> It seems like the ISP1040 isn't compatible
> with the ISP1020. Before I go out and buy a new 
> SCSI controller so I can run Linux on the machine,
> is this true? And if not, what am I doing wrong /
> how can I fix it??

Well, they seem to be mostly compatible, it's just the 1020 firmware
that gets downloaded by the driver that causes problems on the 1040B,
sometimes.

As per the recommendation by John Jemiolo, recompiling the driver to NOT
download its firmware seems to help in many cases.  Letting the SRM/ARC
consoles download *their* firmware to the card seems to help, as the driver
will successfully use the 1040B under that condition.

However, this is not real helpful to you when you're not yet installed...

Unfortunately, there's no "canned" kernel that behaves the way you need; my
suggestion would be to find an old IDE disk and CDROM, and install a minimal
system using them, under which you can build an appropriately patched kernel.

Sorry, and good luck.

--Jay++

