From: "Scott Spieker" <scspieker@ncweb.com> Mon, 8 Jun 1998

> 1.  I am assuming that the ttyS0 port is the correct tty line for the port
> - is that indeed correct?
Yes, that's correct.

> 2.  Enabling the serial port in the kernel is done in what module(s)? 
> During the make config step what is the question that is posed regarding
> the serial port/terminal options?
Enabling in the kernel is done by enabling Serail Ports. The default is
usually to have them enabled.
I was thinking that you should add a line in /etc/inittab like:
# Run gettys in standard runlevels
1:12345:respawn:/sbin/mingetty tty1
...
7:12345:respawn:/sbin/getty ttyS0 DT19200 vt100

Item # 7. But set speed and terminal type to match your terminal.
This will enable logins on your serial port. You also have to add
ttyS0 to the /etc/securetty file to enable root logins on this port.


> 3.  Setting the terminal as the console can be done with the OSLOADOPTIONS
> with milo.  Is there a different place that that information should be
> installed at also?
/dev/console is usually a link to /dev/tty or /dev/tty0 - which are the
ttys for the standard screen/keyboard. If you want console messages to
come to your serial port, you would make /dev/console a link to ttyS0.
You'd probably be better of logging the messages you're interested in
to /var/log/messages (by editing /etc/syslog.conf).

There is also an option in the kernel config to enable/disable logging
to the serial port - this is mainly useful at boot time if you don't
have a monitor connected. It can cause problems if you have a modem
on this port, but a terminal should be okay.

> I have to admit that I have gotten away from Linux for a short while and
> became mush more intimate with the commercial versions of UNIX.  That is
The RedHat stuff mostly follows standard SysV practices for init,config,etc.,
, so probably is somewhat like commercial UNIX - depending on which
UNIX flavor you use. SunOS is a BSD type system, but Solaris is SysV, so
I've been able to give the guys at work a few tips on setting up Solaris.

Larry Z.



