I used to work in an environment with SunOS (on Sun3 and SPARC), Ultrix (on
Vax, MIPS and Alpha), AIX (on RT and RS/6000) and HP-SUX (on PA-RISC).  We had
our home directories NFS mounted on all of those machines, and used YP (now
NIS) to serve the passwords.  A shell script that tested for the existance of
an the output of mach, arch, uname, etc, did the machine specific set up, and
everybody sourced it out of their .cshrc or .profiles.  Mostly, it was a case
of making sure you had machine specific binaries in your ~/bin.<machinetype>
directory, and the independant stuff in ~/bin.  Same with our product
development tree - "make" would make the binaries into $TREE/bin.<machinetype>
while the shell scripts and perl would be in $TREE/bin.


