On Tue, Jun 09, 1998 at 12:42:54PM -0400, Greg Lindahl wrote:
> On an Alpha, the problem is that the boot info is in the PROM, which I
> can only change by typing on the keyboard. Is there any way to:
> 
> 1) have Milo read the initial args out of a file and set the prom args
> to be something like "=FILENAME", or
> 
> 2) poke the initial args into the Milo executable
> 
> If you don't see why I don't just type the boot args into each
> machine, consider N = 64. Or N = 128.

The Debian/Alpha team has, literally *today*, decided to look into extending
MILO with this sort of functionality.

On Debian/i386, you can compile a custom kernel and have it installed as a
.deb package, and even have some basic management of your boot menu done.

This can't be done (at least, the last bit) on the Alpha because of the way
MILO gets its arguments and the basic impedence mismatch between keeping the
kernel version as part of the filename, etc. We've decided to try and get to
the point where you can have a single boot entry in your ARC console or
whatnot, and have MILO present you with a menu or a prompt like LILO or
*something*, based on the contents of a file in one of the filesystems---we
are planning to allow you to compile in a filename, or specify it from ARC,
etc.

So we're working on this.  We'll keep people assessed.  We'll make our mods
available.

Mike.


