The problem with raid under linux is that there is no way to have the 
kernel boot up and directly recoginze the raid drives. Some user level 
programmes must be run.  I may have missed some recient changes in the 
raid software but as far as I know the only way to do this is through the 
initrd type process. 

> disk. That's a 99% solution for most people; a running system will
> survive a disk failure, but may not be able to reboot.

A raid1 mirrored device is readable by milo and can be used for booting. 
it is just that if you write the drive you are up the creek or if the 
drive is striped.

one other possiblity would be to have a system call that would allow the 
root to be remounted on another device after the system starts it's run 
up. Reading from the the raw device that is part of a mirror is ok it is 
just when you want to mount the file system for writing that you will 
have to have the real /dev/md up and running.

I have even thought a bit about chroot-ing the system after it starts up 
but to do that I would have to hack init.

> initrd in the kernel wouldn't help you; initrd in milo would get the
> other 1%.

