If the kernel is on the FAT partition, then it will work. As you say, this is
not a full solution, the problem being that in order to get that other 1%, you
need to have the BIOS understand the RAID system, and if that is in software,
then it is not going to happen.

You could of course use two discs with identical FAT partitions on them, so 
you are then able intervene and boot off the other one in case of a single disc
failure.

I actually have the kernel on our UDB setup with the kernel in the MILO FAT
partition, which is mounted as /boot. This was only because it seemed like the
right thing to do until I realised better, but why change it when it works ;-)

Alan

