I run Linux on a couple Alphas and a Sparc. The recent Expo made me realize
that Linux development for the Intel platform appears far ahead of that for the
"exotics".

When I got home I bought an (Intel) notebook and installed Linux on it, in order
to be able to play with the Intel version of Linux. The kernel was virtually
completely modularized and worked very well, and the pcmcia support also seemed
far advanced from when I played with it on a UDB with the first Craftworks AXP
release (ie: it actually worked).

What is the status of loadable module support on the alpha platform? Does it
exist in the 5.1 release?

If not, are there any fundamental problems preventing it? Could a
marginally-competent Alpha user like me help make it happen?

It seems to me that the RH distributions are diverging more and more (with the
Sparc really falling behind). I'd like to see them become closer and not
farther away from each other.

Considering that Linus mentioned the Alpha when asked about his favorite
platform, I'd really like to see it catch up with the Intel stuff, both with the
free and the commercial apps.

Just my $0.02, and I'm *still* looking for a good Word Processor for my Alpha
machines :-)

---
Brian E.W. Wood  beww@intac.com


