Subject: More assembler stuff
In-Reply-To: <3.0.5.32.19980605091857.0083a480@ellpspace.math.ualberta.ca>
X-Status: N
X-Mailer: Applixware 4.3 (807.65.0.22)

To answer the few questions I had regarding compiler, I had tested the code 
with gcc and egcs 1.02 and the results were that a little fiddling with the 
assembler code gave a big improvement in performance.  I was advised by Steven 
Tweedie (who happens to be based here in Edinburgh and was at our local Linux 
group meeting last week) to try 1.03a with haifa enabled.  This I did and the 
result was the code ran at the same speed as before, in fact I could not see 
any difference in the output from 1.02 and 1.03a.  Most scary though, 
optimisations higher than -O resulted in the code being slower as with 1.02.

I think we will continue hand optimising for the moment.  We can't release the 
source code because it is a commercial product but I can say that one of the 
tricks we used on the SGI to get better performance was to duplicate all 
variables in our inner loop and double the amount of work which gave the SGI 
compiler the clue to re-order instructions etc and gave it a better chance of 
using both pipelines.  This resulted in the SGI code running about 40% quicker 
than it had previously.  This same code on the alpha obviously produces code 
which doesn't fill both pipelines because the compiler doesn't take the hint so 
we hand tweak the code and get the same result as with the SGI, about 30-40% 
speedup.

Of course, we are still only about 20% faster than the SGI while running a 
clock which is nearly triple the speed of our R10000s.  No doubt we will do 
better when our LX motherboard arrives to replace the SX we didn't order but 
even so......

Steven assures me that the 21264 will solve all our problems and is 
blisteringly fast, since we're hoping to sell a software solution based on a 
commercially available budget box (and the alpha/linux combination is the prime 
candidate) this may well be what we need but we would still like to get the 
best out of the 21164, at least we're getting somewhere with this.  Our problem 
uses virtually no floating point, the code runs in around 1MB of RAM too and we 
are not running out of registers either.

I agree though, alphas are better than SGIs :-D

Dr. Shane Sturrock - sss@holyrood.ed.ac.uk
