Subject: Ultra-2 SCSI
Mime-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="us-ascii"

We have been experimenting with Ultra-2 SCSI, aka Low Voltage Differential
(LVD).

Ultra-2, for those not familiar, is a low-cost, low current version of
differential SCSI, which has some nice features:

40MHz clock rate, which translates to 80MB/sec transfers ( as opposed to 40
for Ultra-1 Wide, 20 for Ultra-1 narrow, and 10 for Fast SCSI).
As it is a differential schem, rather than single-ended, it supports 15
devices, on about 30 feet of cable, with no termination problems, or other
of the classic high speed SCSI intrinsic nasties.
The new Adaptec and Symbios Logic cards are out that support this, and the
Intraserver is slated to ship in July.

Drives are now available from the major makers, such as Quantum, Seagate,
and Fujitsu. Costs are similar to regular Wide Ultra disks.

What was needed:

For Symbios Logic 53C895 chip based cards a new version of the Symbios
Logic 53c8xx driver is on ftp://ftp.tux.org/pub/roudier/
Version 2.6n is what seems to work so far.

For a milo  Jay Estabrook cooked one and we have been using it with success.
We built our own 2.0.33 kernel, and are running tests on an LX 533MHz machine.

It seems to work nicely.

Now, a caution:
If you are looking for fast transfers on single disk systems, this won't
help you much. Current disk drives can do maybe 20MB/sec data rates, so an
80 per sec bus data rate won't show much difference. It will show up on
multi-disk configurations, where we don't want the controller or the bus to
be the bottleneck. In bonnie benchmarks we only see a marginal difference
on a single disk. So, this is only going to gain you a performance boost of
useful value if you are running at least 2 or more disks on the bus.

Those who may be interested please contact me, and we will see about
getting the needed files available for you.

BTW, if I am tardy in responding to questions, we will be away at USENIX
from June 14th to 21st.



