A few comments here, from someone who has done "generic" kernel work (I was
in the Digital UNIX group for 3 years), and who is contemplating it again
for LINUX:

1. autodetection of hardware isn't always that easy; even the CPU "registers"
   aren't always in the same place. Yes, this is what "amask" and "implver"
   are supposed to help with, but there's more to it than that.

2. DU depends on SRM console setting up the a certain data structure (HWRPB)
   which contains much critical information; the latest MILOs now do
   a much better job, making it possible to contemplate "generic" kernels
   booted from ARC/AlphaBIOS.

3. have you seen what it *costs*? DU's genvmunix was over 11Mb, last I looked.
   yes, that's largely drivers, but there's a *lot* of machine-specific code
   in there, too... :-\

--Jay++

