> away to unmount filesystems and reboot. When it attempts to reboot it reports
> block 0 of dkb0.0.0.6.0 is not a valid boot block. bootstrap failure.
> 
> the command it has tried to run is:
> 
> boot dkb0.0.0.6.0 -file 2/vmlinux.gz -flags A
> 
> The second time through the install process, we left 1 cylinder on an RZ28 up
> front for the aboot loader (about 2 Mb). Same errors.
> 
> show boot_file reports nothing
> show boot_flags reports A
> 
> set boot_file "2/vmlinux.gz" has no effect.
> 
> Still reports block 0 as invalid boot block.

I guess the install-program didn't install a bootloader for you

Boot from floppy once again with

boot dva0 -file vmlinux.gz -flags "root=/dev/sda??"
Where sda?? is your root-partition.

then run

swriteboot /dev/sda /boot/bootlx

abootconf /dev/sda 2

This works only if you used bds-disklabel style partitions and have a 
number of free sectors at the start of the disk.
Otherwise, you could leave the floppy in an instruct the machine to get
its kernel from the floppy. 

Good Luck,

Edwin

