[...]
> Face it, some people just aren't cut out to run Linux.  I say, rather than

It is only sane to agree with the first statement.  

> dumbing down Linux or engaging in excessive hand-holding for these people, we
> say "fine, run NT, it's your funeral" and cut them loose.  Or refer them to
> people who install and support Linux for money.
> 

However, if we *ever* expect Linux to be taken seriously this needs to 
be reworded.  Responses like this only serve to substantiate those that 
condemn Linux as an elitist hacker-toy O/S.  At the very least we should
encourage folks like this to explain what went wrong.  Then if it's 
in the FM, point 'em to the right spot and suggest that they RTFM.  
This isn't handholding, it's common courtesy.  If someone walks up to you 
on the street and expresses some dismay at the layout of your city do 
you dismiss them as not smart enough to navigate the streets, therefore 
they don't merit a helpful response?  I dare say that wouldn't do much 
for what people thought of your community.  Keep in mind that Linux
is a community as well.  Linux has been continually "dumbed 
down" in various areas. Early versions of Red Hat/Intel required you to 
know your hardware in fairly good detail so you could pick the correct 
boot image out of 72 choices,  then you had to rawrite or dd it to floppy.  
Now they supply a *single* boot disk that works for over 95% of the people 
that try it.  Is this "dumbing down" or progress?  The first time I 
installed SLS in 1993 I had to reconfigure every device in my system but 
the video card in order for the drivers to locate my SCSI controller.
If this is dumbing down, I'll take it.  Installing an Alpha is leaps and
bounds easier than SLS ever was.  That doesn't mean Linux has been dumbed 
down.  If someone still has problems finding a clue after some initial 
help, then you should probably nudge them *gently* back to NT, but don't
slam the door on them or they're likely to never come back.  Making 
comments such as "fine, run NT, it's your funeral" will only be counter-
productive in the long run.



--Kit


