> I've just finished compiling 2.0.34 with Jay's patches, and all went
> primarily without incident.  Here's the output of stderr:
> 
> osf_sys.c:763: warning: implicit declaration of function `rdfpcr'
> osf_sys.c:765: warning: implicit declaration of function `wrfpcr'
> irq.c: In function `init_IRQ':
> irq.c:1174: warning: unused variable `temp'
> lca.c: In function `lca_machine_check':
> lca.c:474: warning: unknown escape sequence `\p'
> lca.c: In function `lca_clock_print':
> lca.c:520: warning: unsigned int format, different type arg (arg 2)
> lca.c:521: warning: unsigned int format, different type arg (arg 2)

Hmm, I'll look into these, see if I can fix them up.

> Now, LCA has been bugging me lately - it seems that I have some
> questionable memory, and lca_machine_check keeps spewing out errors
> whenever this memory is accessed.  As long as it is corrected (it usually
> is) I don't want it to get thrown to my screen, nor do I want it to get
> logged to syslog.
> 
> Is there,
>   a) A method to mark this memory "bad/used" at boot time?
> or
>   b) A way to shut off the error messages (by commenting stuff out)?
> 

Well, there's easy and there's hard...

Easy: you can tell MILO to limit your memory to a certain amount; this however
is only helpful if the bad memory is at the top of the total installed.

Hard: either modify the kernel to not utilize the offending pages (someplace in
the arch/alpha/mm sources), or modify the LCA machine check handling to ignore
correctible memory errors. The "vector" argument is the machine check "code"
from the table at the end.

Good luck.

--Jay++

