> Quoting Magnus@TIC.Thyssen.com (Magnus@TIC.Thyssen.com): 

> Face it, some people just aren't cut out to run Linux.  I say, rather
> than dumbing down Linux or engaging in excessive hand-holding for these
> people, we say "fine, run NT, it's your funeral" and cut them loose.  Or
> refer them to people who install and support Linux for money. 

Hi -  I'm not going to say "this is the wrong attitude" (even though the
comment *screams* for it to be said) but there is something more important
here than people being "cut out" to run Linux or not.  Now, I'm not going
to evangelize, but especially on the Alpha getting things up and running
can be pretty difficult.  Tolerance in this arena is demanded and
demanding, but critical.  The effort is to get people past the
installation obstacles and *using* the system.  If this means a
dumbed-down installation method, so be it.  Personally, I would love to
see an X based installation of the entire system just like Windows has.
Uhh, scratch that.  Mozilla is free now - Just like Solaris has (but
better).  There's no technical reason this can't be done now.

However, I do think that now is not the time to concentrate on
installation for newbies.  Yes, everybody is a newbie at one point, but
the people we need in the Linux and Linux/Alpha community are developers
and other active willing participants; people who are willing to put in
the time and effort to make a production system and have the gaul to use
it as a primary platform, to push the system and debug in whatever
capacity they can the problems.  People are talking about Linux as an
alternative OS.  For me it is - it's the only home OS I have.  But it's
not ready for that critical mass, and right now we need to entice the
people who can make it that system to get involved.

Well, I guess I did evangelize, but in brief summary, help those who are
willing to help themselves (and the `opensource' community) but also help
the users.  Everybody needs help, and if it doesn't detract from your own
projects, you (anyone reading) should take out a moment or two and help
someone get started.  In time, they will be as proficient as most anyone
in getting a system to boot, and your time investment in them will pay for
itself when they help someone else get started.

All that, and write a FAQ.

regards,
--
Christopher Curtis               - http://www.ee.fit.edu/users/ccurtis
Florida Institute of Technology  - System Administrator, Programmer
Melbourne, Florida  USA          - http://www.lp.org/


