On Thu, Jun 18, 1998 at 09:42:04AM +0200, Joakim Roubert wrote:
> On Wed, 17 Jun 1998, Jon Simantov wrote:
> > Anyone try the Debian 2.0 Alpha distribution? Comments? Suggestions? I'd
> > rather not go the way of Red Hat when I'm so used to Debian... 
> So Debian 2.0 is released both for i386 and alpha?

I'll take the last question first:

Debian 2.0 is going to be coming out for both the i386 and the alpha and
m68k maybe PPC.  The non-i386 releases will lag somewhat compared to the
i386 release, but the alpha and m68k releases (and possibly the PPC
releases, I'm not sure) are already totally useable and stable and largely
complete---the alpha port is currently about 400 packages shy of the 2000 or
so packages Debian/i386 contains.

Of course, not all of those will need to get ported---some are intrinsically
i386, while others are old an have never been made 64-bit clean and are
often simply too specialized and marginal that unless a motivated person
came along who _needed_ it, it might not be seen as a worthwhile investment
of time.

Incidentally, the sparc version is going to probably wait for 2.1, because
we're going straight to glibc for it---which, I'm given to understand, means
we have to wait for glibc 2.1 to be released, as 2.0 apparently isn't ready
for the sparc.  That may be the situation with the PPC port.

Oh, and someone's working on a StrongARM port.  It's up and running I
believe, but I don't know more than that.

For the first question:

I've used Debian/Alpha for more than a year---in fact I only had RedHat on
my machine long enough to bootstrap Debian onto it.  For a long time I had
the only functioning Debian/Alpha development machine.

It has been totally stable.  I've never had to reinstall the OS, despite
having _constantly_ been on the bleeding edge of development and moving the
installation to larger disks no less than five times.

I use this machine every day---it is my primary work machine, I use it for
administering a group of other machines running Solaris and Debian/i386.
I've swapped hard drives, I've swapped video cards (I've got a UDB), I
backup to tape, I read mail, I use X, I run an NFS server and use ssh and
have some private FTP stuff and never had any problems, even when, say, a
recompile of X has the machine very busy otherwise.

You should definitely look into it.  You can join the debian/alpha mailing
list by sending mail to debian-alpha-request@lists.debian.org with
'subscribe' in the subject.  You can also find archives at www.debian.org.

Mike.


