khan,


I haven't really been following this thread.  I have never ever replied to
a politically motivated topic.  I refuse to flame you.  Its a waste of
time and it tends to turn people off.  To simply "cut them loose" is to
destroy linux.  It has to continue to grow.  It has to continue to become
easier to use.  

It is still new compared to NT when you think about
the money that is thrown at NT by Microsoft.  I work with just about every
platform now: Solaris, AIX, Irix (soon), DU 4.x, and Linux on Sparc,
Intel, and Alpha.  I still prefer Linux because it is probably the best
damned learning tool for programmers and systems administrators.  It has
two excellent desktops coming down the pipe that will appeal to the
business side (fingers crossed): KDE and GNOME.


I think your anaylysis of RedHat accepting money for a product that works
isn't quite correct.  Linux/Unix has always required a fair amount of
knowledge about systems and what goes into setting them up properly.
Linux runs on too many platforms for it to be perfect. <kudos to the
community>  Besides, there are numerous places you can obtain the
distribution for less than $30.  I think one even just "gives" the CD away
at $1.50?  I typically pay $90+ for my Windows 9x/NT upgrades.  

On that same train of thought, Redhat needs to make a profit.  In my
opinion, they have an excellent distribution model.  You don't HAVE to buy
Redhat.  You can do an FTP install if you have the bandwidth.  They hire
very interesting people like Rasterman, Mandrake<?>, etc to further cool
linux projects.  So from what I can tell, my Redhat distribution did not
go up in price.  It now has more capability than ever.  After seeing GNOME
at the LinuxExpo, I can't wait to see how far they will take it over the
next 9-12 months.

I can go on about all of the development projects in the works: DCE, LDAP,
KDE, GNOME, all the platforms it runs on (PalmPilot).  Not all by Redhat,
true.  The $49 version (seen it for much much less) contains a clearly
understandable book covering installation.  That book was done by
technical writers at Redhat if I remember what Bob Hart (Redhat Support
Manager?) said at the Expo.  Its boxed up pretty and targets the newbie.
Alpha is much newer than the Intel and not as easy.  I followed the
directions and didn't have any problems with the 2 FTP installs I have
done.

khan, ease up, Redhat, Debian, SUSE, Slackware, are working to bring us as
bug-free and secure a product as they can with really small staffs.  They
really don't charge much for the distributions.

I am not willing to say that some people just shouldn't run _insert OS_.
All software has a target audience.  I wish you luck with NT.  I
personally haven't found it to be stable at $149.  I have friends that run
machines for weeks and weeks with NT.  *shrug* 

In closing, keep your eye on Linux.  I firmly believe the best stuff is
yet to come.  

BTW, I am American and I have problems with my language.  No
need to apologize for language difficulties. :)  Quite a few Koreans think
my Hangul is horrible. :)

Scott Stancil
s.e.stancil@larc.nasa.gov
757.864.7365

