Date: Thu, 18 Jun 1998 10:08:04 -0700
From: "Gary A. Allen" <gallen@ironbark.arc.nasa.gov>
Message-Id: <199806181708.KAA00797@ironbark.arc.nasa.gov>
To: axp-list@redhat.com
Subject: Syslogd / Klogd bug

Just thought I'd comment on the Syslogd and Klogd problem.  The basic problem
is when one of these daemons suddenly "goes nuts" and ties up more-and-more
of the system's resources leading to a system crash.   You can see the crash
coming if you run Top and notice that Syslogd and/or Klogd are growing in
CPU usage.

I do not have a solution to this bug but I'm offering a clue.  Normally
I run Syslogd and Klogd without trouble.  However the bug seems to triggered
when our network security people scan our local networks for security holes.
Their procedure is to run a script file that attacks our machines with
all known system hacks.  If they are able to break into a person's machine
then that person gets scolded for being lax on security and told to fix
the security hole.  This Alpha Syslogd bug is NOT a security hole and our
network security guys have not flagged it as such.  However their security
sweeps do trigger this problem.

It maybe(?) that other people who are experiencing this Syslogd bug are also
having their machines attacked by local network security people or genuine
hackers.

                                 Gary


