Dear khan,

First let me assure you that I find it completely awful that you could get
a reply such as you did.

As to your points, let me tell you my own recent experience with a PW500a,
a close raltive to your PW433a.

I purchased it in what the French digital dealer (ESC Informatique) calls a
"double boot NT/Linux configuration".  That meant that it was delivered
with a 1G partition on the disk containing Windows NT 4.0 [1] and a "RedHat
5.0 for Alpha" boxed set together with ten sheets of fax'ed instructions
for installing Red Hat on the system (in French).

I followed the instructions, and after a few tries it could install except
that the disk was very slow, and there was no network.  I naturally blocked
the payment of the machine (since it was not functioning as purchased) and
then

(1) informed the dealer that it would not run,

(2) sent a bug report to red hat, and

(3) asked on the axp-list@redhat.com.

I had many replies, including "did you plug it in" and even less respectful
ones, like you.  However, I also had very helpful replies from Jay
Estabrook (digital) and Loic Prylli (the Debian GNU/Linux crowd) that
helped me solve the disk problem (with a kernel patch) and gave me input to
track down the network bug.  However, no useful replies came from Red Hat
itself (so I still have technical support, I suppose, since my bug "ticket"
is unclosed :).  Thus Loic's replies made me install Debian GNU/Linux 2.0
for Alpha and this is where the problem was eventually solved, after many
hours of work [2].

The good point here is that (almost) everybody where actually very helpful,
including digital who had an engineer out for a whole day trying to
establish whether the fault was a hardware one (replacing basically
everything in the box).

The bad point is that Red Hat 5.0 Alpha did not work, even though the
machine is listed among the recognised ones (a PW 433/500 a/au is known as
a "Miata", as you surely know).  So it certainly was not ready for the
general audience, as you have pointed out.

So you are right: the Red Hat product should have carried the label "beta"
or something like that.  My personal feeling is that Red Hat has been too
succesful: if "normal" people know about Linux, then they know about Red
Hat.  And Red Hat, like is the case in almost all such cases, has not been
able to handle the success.

I think the release of 5.0, and the related experience, will be a cold
shower for Red Hat; let us hope that they emerge refreshed and humble so
they can get on to solve the problems in a serious way!

As for the French digital dealer, I think they acted in good faith.
However, for them it should be obvious that installing GNU/Linux on the
machines in advance would weed out the problems in a much cheaper way
(since centralised installation is a much simpler task).  And perhaps also
just considering non-Red Hat solutions such as Debian would spark Red Hat
to not rely too much on the inertia of their success.

Sincerely,
	    Kristoffer

Notes.

[1] I see absolutely no reason to abuse products sold by other companies by 
    writing the product name in creative ways.  If GNU/Linux is better then
    that's what we should advertise!

[2] It turned out that the problem was with the auto-sensing of one
    particular hub entry was buggy in connection with the Linux driver
    not insisting on explicitly specified parameters.

[3] Many other products should carry such a label but that, again, is not
    the point here.

-- 
