Kristoffer, khan, et al:

	Here in the US, we ar lucky that we have many fine DEC Resellers 
that offer similar systems (dual boot WinNT/Red Hat Linux) - but in our
market these dealers install the software prior to shipping the
hardware,
and in those cases, the dealers detect all manner of hardware/software
incompatabilities before the customer ever sees the machine.

	If I was given a machine that was labeled as "dual-boot" but
only had one OS installed, I would have been angry with the dealer
BEFORE I ever opened the Red Had Shrinkwrapped box! The machine you
got was dual-boot CAPABLE.

	I would point my frustration at the Dealer who sold you less
than you ordered, and could not prepare the machine to suit your needs.

	But, there is also a small amount of frustration that can be
pointed at Red Hat, for the number of bug/problems with RH 5.0 AXP, 
but they made pathes available onthe Web, and when your dealer set
your box up, they would be expected to install those updates prior to
delivery at your company.

	Red Hat is not innocent, but the dealer is guilty as well.

	What if the situation was the other way around, and your system
came with Red Hat Linux installed correctly, but WinNT came in a box
with 10 pages of FAXed instructions for installation - what would you
think of that dealer? About Microsoft? (I bet the WinNT install had
the latest patches installed (Service Pack 3?) and maybe even FX!32...)

	That is how I see it, and I suspect that is how most others see it
as well, but I could be way off the mark here...

Ken
khansen@njcc.com

