> You know, the math for this *really* isn't that hard.  1024 * 768 * (24 bit
> per pixel/8 bits per byte) = 2359296 bytes.  Therefore you can do that res at
> 24 bit TrueColor with slightly more than 2Mb.

Well, considering that 1+1=2, I must agree.

> With 24bit color (there is no
> reason in the world to have more than 24 bit color - that's why it's called
> TrueColor), you can get 1600x1280 or even 2000x1600 (if your monitor could go
> that high) in 4Mb.  Or you could do lower res, and use double buffering if the
> hardware (and X) supports it.

I've often wondered about this. Any idea why they make 32 bit and 48 bit
cards? My cynical side says its just a way to make the numbers bigger so
that clueless people buy that particular card, but I wonder if there is
a justification for it. 

I'm no biologist, but it seems to me that 2^24 is more colors than I'm
going to see, or at least consciously distinguish. Hell.. when I've 
edited 24 bit images I've found that at times, the computer says that
there are two different colors present, but I'll be damned if I could
tell the difference... and I zoomed down to 320x200 to get a better
view too.

