>                                                        1024 * 768 * (24 bit
> per pixel/8 bits per byte) = 2359296 bytes.  Therefore you can do that res at
> 24 bit TrueColor with slightly more than 2Mb.

But since an expansion board returns data in chunks the size of the
bus connection, a PCI card is actually send/receiving data 4 or 8
bytes at a time (depending on the PCI implementation).  It seems it
would be more time-efficient to store that way in the video ram, but
I don't know whether it's done that way, since a board might have
ticks available, and it would involve some extra effort in the
firmware or circuit logic.

I'm not expert, but I'm not confident that your math is correct; at
any rate, it's not -obviously- correct, as you suggest it should be.

> 24 bit TrueColor with slightly more than 2Mb.  With 24bit color (there is no
> reason in the world to have more than 24 bit color - that's why it's called
> TrueColor),

I can think of at least one, in addition to the above.  It's called
alpha channel, and it's conceivable that you'd want a piece of
hardware to handle it for you.  I suspect it's called "True Color"
because some marketing genius thought it would make the boxes get off
the shelves faster.

>             you can get 1600x1280 or even 2000x1600 (if your monitor

