Dear Ken,

> Here in the US, we ar lucky that we have many fine DEC Resellers that
> offer similar systems (dual boot WinNT/Red Hat Linux) - but in our market
> these dealers install the software prior to shipping the hardware, and in
> those cases, the dealers detect all manner of hardware/software
> incompatabilities before the customer ever sees the machine.

You are very fortunate, then :)

> If I was given a machine that was labeled as "dual-boot" but only had one
> OS installed, I would have been angry with the dealer BEFORE I ever
> opened the Red Had Shrinkwrapped box! The machine you got was dual-boot
> CAPABLE.

You are right - this is exactly what the dealer here should have done.

> I would point my frustration at the Dealer who sold you less than you
> ordered, and could not prepare the machine to suit your needs.

I hope they take the hint (and realize how gentle I was... :-).

> But, there is also a small amount of frustration that can be pointed at
> Red Hat, for the number of bug/problems with RH 5.0 AXP, but they made
> pathes available onthe Web, and when your dealer set your box up, they
> would be expected to install those updates prior to delivery at your
> company.

Indeed - the major advantage of a product such as Red Hat is that it
facilitates centralised setup - such as can be performed by dealers.

> Red Hat is not innocent, but the dealer is guilty as well.

I agree.

> What if the situation was the other way around, and your system came with
> Red Hat Linux installed correctly, but WinNT came in a box with 10 pages
> of FAXed instructions for installation - what would you think of that
> dealer? About Microsoft? (I bet the WinNT install had the latest patches
> installed (Service Pack 3?) and maybe even FX!32...)

Well...I would not have noticed (but I am sure that someone else would :).

> That is how I see it, and I suspect that is how most others see it as
> well, but I could be way off the mark here...

I agree, in principle.  But it remains a fact that in Europe the customer
is not king.  The good consequence is that you can run a business without a
huge risk of (or insurance premium againts) being sued.  The flip-side is
that you cannot do as much in cases like this (were part of my motivation
was also to SUPPORT that they actually DO sell me Linux instead of ignoring 
it completely).

This is a dilemma that we have to take seriously if we want Linux to
succeed: we MUST permit Linux to have the same business startup problems as 
other products (do *you* remember Windows version 2?).

Cheers,
	Kristoffer

