mgraffam@mhv.net said:
> I've often wondered about this. Any idea why they make 32 bit and 48
> bit cards? My cynical side says its just a way to make the numbers
> bigger so that clueless people buy that particular card, but I wonder
> if there is a justification for it.

I think that the 32bit modes are 24bit with an 8bit pad. This is a little
easier on the host processor, I think. As for 48?

mgraffam@mhv.net said:
> I'm no biologist, but it seems to me that 2^24 is more colors than I'm
> going to see, or at least consciously distinguish.

Color is more complicated then that. Compter graphics is based on tristimulus
(RGB) metamers. In other words, the computer screen simply cannot come
even remotely close to rendering the colors of the real world. However,
by an accident of biology, there are actually huge sets of colors that
produce similar perceptions. A large set of those perceptions can be stimulated
by combining three (or more) well chosen colors.

All this is well known, but often forgotten:-)

What is slightly less well known is that our ability to see chrominance
detail is really quite poor. Most of our ability to see detail comes from
our perception of luminance. In practice, a 16bit palette color system would
be very generous. Most any scene can be rendered very nicely with 64K
colors, if those colors are well chosen.

24bit color is easier to work with because we can put less effort into
figuring out what colors are really too similar to seen. But We can barely
see 256 shades of gray. If you have a decent process, and the X server that
supports it, 16bit palette color would be the very best. (Less memory used,
plenty of colors to see.)

YCrCb better reflects our capacity to perceive what is represented, expecially
if the Cr and Cb planes are subsampled. Monitors, however, are controlled
with RGB values so gamma corrections to support monitor idiosynchracies is
best done in RGB space.
