	Although I'm sure that the economic incentive does exist the reasons
actually go much deeper than this.  The most basic reason is simply time.=

For Red Hat to make an rpm "theirs" someone inside the organization has
to look over the spec file, locate and grab the pristine sources, =

check out the patches (if any) and then rebuild the rpm.  Add to =

this the daunting task of just keeping track of which version of =

which program is current and the level of complexity becomes excessive
in short order.  Implementing such a plan could easily consume one
person, perhaps two at times.  Not to mention the potential for boredom
if your job description only read "rpm builder."  :-)  =


Support is another closely related issue.  Supporting the boxed set =

with updates gives a (usually) small set of variables to contend with.
Contrib rpms just aren't supported, and to constantly roll-over the =

rpms would provide support folks with a moving target that could be =

next to impossible to hit.

The other major issue is one that was mentioned earlier, major upgrades.
Most of the time these are best left for when you have a known quantity
to deal with.  Trying to update significant components (libraries, kernel=
,
etc) on-the-fly can have a negative impact on things.  =



--Kit


