On Sun, 21 Jun 1998, Paul Tomblin wrote:

> scsi0 channel 0 : resetting for second half of retries.
> SCSI bus is being reset for host 0 channel 0.
> scsi0 : DCMD|DBC=0x50000000, DNAD=0x40290cf8 (virt 0xfffffc0000290cf8)
>          DSA=0x45d96214 (virt 0xfffffc0005d96214)
>          DSPS=0x40290e08, TEMP=0x40290988 (virt 0xfffffc0000290988), DMODE=0x80
>          SXFER=0x0, SCNTL3=0x13
>          phase=MSGIN, 0 bytes in SCSI FIFO
>          STEST0=0x7
> scsi0 : DSP 0x40290cf0 (virt 0xfffffc0000290cf0) ->
> 0x40290cf0 (virt 0xfffffc0000290cf0) : 0x50000000 0x40290e08 (virt 0xfffffc0000290e08)
> 0x40290cf8 (virt 0xfffffc0000290cf8) : 0x60000200 0x00000000 (virt 0x0000000000000000)
> 0x40290d00 (virt 0xfffffc0000290d00) : 0x78388000 0x00000000 (virt 0x0000000000000000)
> 0x40290d08 (virt 0xfffffc0000290d08) : 0x0f000001 0x40290668 (virt 0xfffffc0000290668)
> 0x40290d10 (virt 0xfffffc0000290d10) : 0x78389000 0x00000000 (virt 0x0000000000000000)
> 0x40290d18 (virt 0xfffffc0000290d18) : 0xc0000004 0x40290664 (virt 0xfffffc0000290664) 0x00008834 (virt 0x0000000000000000)
> scsi0 : issue queue
> scsi0 : schedule dsa array :
> scsi0 : end schedule dsa array
> scsi0 : reconnect_dsa_head :
> scsi0 : end reconnect_dsa_head
> scsi0: lost command 1994
> scsi0 : target 3 ignored SDTR and went into COMMAND OUT
> scsi : aborting command due to timeout : pid 1994, scsi0, channel 0, id 3, lun 0 Start/Stop Unit 00 00 00 03 00 
> scsi0 : DANGER : command running, can not abort.

Yes, those are quite concerning messages.  Looks like you have an old or
broken CD-ROM.  Try recompiling one more time, but set
ALLOW_SCSI_DISCONNECT to "no" (if you used "yes").

If that doesn't work, try to force synchronous transfers and failing that,
turn off the fast option.  You may want to make everything safe (hit '?'
when prompted y/n) and then turn things on until you find the right one.

On a side note, and you probably shouldn't do this too often, but seeing
as these are simply options for the controller, there shouldn't be any
dependencies across kernel subdirectory boundaries and you can probably
get away with doing a "make dep" without a "make clean" to save yourself
some compile time.  You should be able to use the same modules as well,
but this is less likely.  Worst case you may have to recompile the
modules for the new kernel.

regards,
