Quoting Keith Baker (toaster@cybercom.net):
> How can I safely copy my root partition to another drive...  last time it
> was on my intel and I used a bit-bit disk copy...  What can I do with
> axp linux...

I've always been a big fan of using tar for this, but I see from the other
messages in this thread that there are "new"[1] arguments to "cp" that do
similar things.  Anyways, the command that worked on Jurassic Unix, and which
still works now, is (starting from where you want to copy it from)

tar cvf - . | (cd /foo/bar; tar xvPf -)

You can also do this from one machine to another if they're set up to allow
rsh between them:

tar cvf - . | rsh foohost "cd /foo/bar; tar xvPf -"

But because this is Unix, and that means there are always a million different
ways of doing anything, I expect some equally old-timer to now chime in and
tell you how to do it using cpio or dd.

[1] Where "new" in this context means "added to Unix some time in the last 10
years since I've stopped having to read the manual to know how to do cp, and
so missed anything new added"

