First off, FAQ fodder.
XF86-SVGA is severely broken when compiled with .40 or .41 egcs, it has
to be compiled with gcc -V 2.7.2.3.  And because the gcc-2.7.2.3
distribution doesn't compile on Alpha, you have to get the Redhat
binaries.

I have severe scheduler problems on my 2.1.106 kernel.  The symptoms are
that keyboard interrupts don't reach the kernel unless something else
does a context switch.  And the timer interrupt does not seem to work,
either.  If I start a kernel compile, the responsiveness is unacceptable
during the compile and your keystrokes only reach the kernel (including
Num-Lock and switching the console!) when output is done in the compile
window.  If I start an MP3 player in the background, responsiveness
greatly increases, probably because the MP3 player creates some context
switches.

Anyway, I just made SSLeay and started the "speed" benchmark of it.
I had, of course, and MP3 player running in the background.  The alarms
stopped reaching the speed process when the MP3 player stopped and I had
to hard-reset the machine.  Even "ping" did not reach the machine
anymore.

By the way: the current glibc does not compile on Alpha.  I'm working on
patches.

Felix


