every gtk-application that uses pixmaps, aborts and shows a 
following error message:

** ERROR **: BadAccess (attempt to access private resource denied)
  serial 7668 error_code 10 request_code 129 minor_code 3

Unfortunately, this doesn't say much to me.

The problem is not with the applications - every gtk-application 
behaves in the same way, but only on alpha. I have compiled the
gtk and the apps on an intel-based system just to be sure, 
and they worked ok.

If, however, I run the apps with "--no-xshm" flag they work. So, 
it looks like the problem is with the shared memory in X. 

I don't remember having problems like this, until I recently 
upgraded the libc using the rpm I found in RedHat's errata,
so it could be something in the libc. I could, of course, run
my apps using the --no-xshm flag, but to my mind circumventing 
a problem instead of solving it, is plain stupid in the long 
run.

The gtk-apps seemed to work slightly better in 2.1.85 as they 
didn't crash all the time - "only" occasionally. Naturally,
this won't do either. Running such programs reminds me too much 
of MS-software: you'd have to have the latest version of the os
and even then the programs would crash periodically. ;-)

So, my question is whether the cause for this problem should be 
hunted for in libc (2.0.7), X (3.3.1), kernel or gtk itself?

