Programmatically, you can force a value to not be overridable, or to set
defaults that can be overridden by the database.  If you arrange the
XAPPLRESDIR path and permissions on the directories correctly, you can have
all sorts of control over what's overridable and what's not.  For instance,
make the ../app-defaults/fixed NFS mounted read-only so that only the system
administrator can change it on a site-wide basis, make the ../app-defaults
directory only writable by the local machine owner, and ~/.Xdefaults writable
by the user.  If you make the "fixed" directory come last in the XAPPLRESDIR
path, it will override anything the users override.

> > The MAIN thing I hate about Windows is the lack of an equivalent to the X
> > Resource Database.
> 
> Does xrdb notify the clients of the changes?

No, but then again you don't have to reboot the whole damn machine every time
you make a change to the database like you do with so many windows programs.

> There's just a few integration issues that are missing ... XFree allows
> you to change resolutions without restarting, but what about color depth?

XFree, probably not.  X does - on SGI I used to have my display set to 8 bit
pseudo colour, but the application I was working on was 24 bit TrueColor.  We
used to force the customers to start their X server as 24 bit TrueColor, but
they wanted to run another application that forced them to start their server
as 32 bit, so it was either lose an important customer or fix our application
to set the visual type for the individual windows.  Guess which one I did?

> I think, for instance, that some commonality would be benficial, like
> common dialog boxes.  However, I would say that these need to be put in
> the window manager, but you need the back-end support from the client.

Possibly.  Especially if they can be cached on the server like pixmaps are.
But not if it would mean that your server forced you into a single
look-and-feel.  Being able to run different window managers and different
upper level apis (Motif, athena, raw Xt) is both a blessing and a curse of X.
Mostly a blessing, I think.

