On Tue, 23 Jun 1998, Jeff Sturm wrote:

> Let's say I render a .gif in one window.  I'ts an 8-bit picture of a 
> rose blossom growing in my backyard.  The hardware colormap gets filled with
> shades of red and green.  Then I open another .gif, a picture of a blue 
> sky.  It wants shades of blue, but no colormap entries are available (they 
> get used on a first-come, first-serve basis).  The results are horrible 
> unless I use private colormaps, in which case the flickering is 
> horrendous but I can view both images (alternately).

    In windows, this isn't a problem.  The Windows kernel handles the
colormap allocations.  So it gives your application the colors it wants
(not always all of them I believe) and uses the rest to display the rest
of the apps.  You're rose would get alot less shades of red and green, but
it's surprising how good the display still looks compared to the freaky
colors you get with X.  And this is all done transparently.  You don't
have to write a single line of code for it to do this.  For most
applications, this gives you much better color handling then the colormap
flashing you get with X.

						- Paul


