They can share. The problem is there is no 'standard' how to do it well.
Using a 16bit True Color visual, I can display as many color 
intensive apps as I want as long as they support True color.
Course that means programmer has to know how to use that visual.
It is different compared to Pseudo-Color.
I doubt Windows is capable of running two simultaneous high
color apps at the same time with BOTH windows visible. Have
you ever noticed that high color windows alway seem to expand
themselves to full screen under Windows? At least the 3-4 I've used
do. Two obvious reasons. One, more pixels, two, can't see what
any other window looks like. Now I don't use Windows alot so
I give them the benefit of doubt that the main reason is #1.
Honestly, I see no easy way to manage 8 bit displays better
than X does. Once you get to 16 bit and a True Color visual,
you have no problems, either with Windows or X. If I think
about it, next time I run Windows, I'll use one of my image
manipulation programs and display two radically different
pics in two simultaneous windows at 8bit and see what it
looks like. Doubt it will be pretty though. One trick I do
in an 8 bit environment is to maintain an atom in the server
window of a set of colormap entries to use for my programs
that guarantees the main WM colors don't flash. Then, any
cell outside that range is copied into the private map. Only
cells in that range can be changed by the apps. Looks a
bit better than the whole damn thing flashing, borders and
all, but again, this is the programmer's responsibility.


My $.02.

Wes


