> ffffffff
>  The Rx process state is 'Stopped'.
>  The Tx process state is 'Stopped'.
> Transmit stopped, Receive stopped, half-duplex.
>  The transmit threshold is 128.
>  Port selection is 100mbps-SYM/PCS 100baseTx scrambler, half-duplex.
> EEPROM contents:
>   0000 0000 0000 0000 0000 0000 0000 0000
>   0000 0103 0000 75f8 779b 1e00 0000 0800
>   8605 0002 08ff 00f0 0286 ff04 f008 8600
>   0102 08ff 00f0 0286 ff02 f008 9100 0003
>   ff02 f008 0000 7800 01e0 5000 1800 0000
>   0000 0000 0000 0000 0000 0000 0000 0000
>   0000 0000 0000 0000 0000 0000 0000 0000
>   0000 0000 0000 0000 0000 0000 0000 a3e0
>  ID CRC 0xe3 (vs. 00), complete CRC f83a13f5.
> EEPROM transceiver/media description for the DC21142 chip.
> 
> eaf node at offset 30, default media type 0800 (Autosense).
>  5 transceiver description blocks:
>   Media 10baseT,  block type 2.
>    Serial transceiver for 10baseT (media type 0).
>     GP pin direction 08ff  GP pin data 00f0.
>   Media 10baseT-Full Duplex,  block type 2.
>    Serial transceiver for 10baseT-Full Duplex (media type 4).
>     GP pin direction 08ff  GP pin data 00f0.
>   Media 10base2,  block type 2.
>    Serial transceiver for 10base2 (media type 1).
>     GP pin direction 08ff  GP pin data 00f0.
>   Media AUI,  block type 2.
>    Serial transceiver for AUI (media type 2).
>     GP pin direction 08ff  GP pin data 00f0.
>   Media MII,  block type 3.
>    MII interface PHY 0 (media type 11).
>  MII PHY found at address 5, status 0x786d.
>  MII PHY #5 transceiver registers:
>    3100 786d 2000 5c01 01e1 0021 0000 0000
>    0000 0000 0000 0000 0000 0000 0000 0000
>    0000 0000 0000 0000 0000 0000 0001 8060
>    8020 0c65 0000 3400 a3b9 0042 cc05 001d.
>   Internal autonegotiation state is 'Ability detect'.
> 

This is a very interesting configuration. Looks like
you have more than one interface. How many connections
on the back of the system? I'd guess 10BT, AUI, 10base2.
Have you posted to Becker's site and asked for help?
Most likely someone there has more knowledge but I
can give you an idea about what can happen. First, the
tulip code appears to have identified at least two
PHY interfaces, one non-MII, the other MII. Or,
a PHY that behaves at least two ways anyway. I've
always had good luck with MII interfaces. So, if
somehow tulip.o tries to use the wrong PHY interface,
you could have problems. There are some relatively 
straight forward loops in tulip.c that scan the leaf
node entries looking for matches. If this were my box,
I'd start the scan from the last node to the first.
I think the default is first to last. Reason being, 
you'll try the MII interface first that way. Problem
is you need to know which connection you're using to
decide the scan order. Tulip.c does check for link beats
on 10BT/100B-TX but those don't exist on AUI/10base2
I believe so Tulip.o has no way to know those are
being used. If there were a robust way of determining
if an interface is connected or not, then the scan
order wouldn't make any difference. Your other option
is the de4x5.c module. It may have the above mentioned
robust scan method. My Netgear FA310TX has only one
leaf node and it's similar to your last entry and works
well with tulip.o.


Wes


