I suspect what Windows does, and what X applications could easily do, is to
divide the entire color space up before hand into as many chunks as your card
would support, and then when an application requests a color, gives it
whichever of the pre-registered colours is the closest match.  I don't know
the best way to allocate those colours - my first inclination on an eight bit
display would be all permutations and combinations of 3 bits for red, 3 green,
2 blue - and then somehow map that back onto 16 bits per channel of the X
colour scheme.  But I'm sure my former collegues at Kodak could come up with
something better.

