ptomblin@xcski.com said:
> I suspect what Windows does, and what X applications could easily do,
> is to divide the entire color space up before hand into as many chunks
> as your card would support, and then when an application requests a
> color, gives it whichever of the pre-registered colours is the closest
> match.

That is exactly what the truecolor visual does under X. Try it. The
results are mixed, although sure enough the colormap switching goes away.
A color allocation will always succeed, but the closest color (that can
be represented in 332) will be returned.


ptomblin@xcski.com said:
> But I'm sure my former collegues at Kodak could come up with something
> better.

"better" requires calculating a histogram of all the colors requested.
A palette can be calculated from that, then the pixels can be mapped onto
that calculated palette.

But then, some application will display a photograph using a gazillion
shades of pink and your green color scheme has left you with only 2 reds:-)

Windows works because the desktop only uses a few colors that are known to
exist in the M$ $tandard palette, and the applications that display images
go through a *great* deal of effort to approximate and dither. High
resolutions help.

This is way off topic, I'll be quiet now.

