> anybody cares about OS/2, in which case they might port it to Alpha as well).

Figuring out what IBM is actually up to tends to be a full time job for people 
*at* IBM let alone anywhere else...

They've got to be starting to worry about the notion of running a 64 bit 
processor.  There's enough hype out there that the marketers at IB have got to 
have noticed that.  And if they're faintly observant, they'll have noticed 
that:
a) PPC is 32 bit, and thus "obviously outdated" (of course, if it's so 
obvious, it is probably a mistaken notion...)
b) From an IBM perspective, Merced is vaporware
c) Alpha is no longer the "enemy" Digital chip the way it was before.

I agree that they're not likely to be moving AS/400's over to Alpha; ditto for 
mainframes and OS/2.  NT obviously already exists for Alpha, and Alpha could 
help resolve some of the scalability issues, or at least *appear* to do so.

And I'll be agnostic about AIX; opinions could readily countervail:
- AIX is already portable to PPC and IA-32;
- If IBM wants there to be a Merced version, it makes sense to work on 
striking down 32-bit-isms now even if an Alpha edition proves commercially 
unviable;
- On the other hand, an Alpha version of AIX would surely take some time to 
produce, thus restricting IBM's ability to market it before Merced is 
available;
- IBM can obviously take a look at Linux/Alpha and BSD/Alpha code, which may 
provide some useful guidance in porting efforts.  Or perhaps deploy Linux or 
NetBSD or OpenBSD.  They could hire Theo de Raadt for what they'd consider 
peanuts; my brother was on a $100M team where, for a while, before he left he 
was the only person producing code and thus generating any actual value for 
IBM...
--
