Martin Kahlert wrote:
> 
> I thought, these convolution stuff should run like a champ
> on Alpha. But since even Digital's Compiler can't do much
> here, i think it's not egcs/gcc's fault.
> (Alex Rhomberg reported for a 500MHz Alpha:
> "DEC C V5.2-036 on Digital UNIX V4.0 (Rev. 564) 168.97 MFLOPS")

That's right. As this convolution consists of floating point
arithmetics only (besides loading / stroring), it should be
fast on the Alpha.

> Perhaps there could be done some tuning for memory
> access on Alphas? But: all arrays should fit in the cache -
> don't they?

I see only a single array in yout code, whose size is
2*1024*sizeof(double) = 16k. Note that the 21164 has a
L1 data cache of 8k. MFLOPS numbers from your code stay
the same with N=512 instead of 1024.

> There should be higher MFlops possible on these machines.
> Perhaps the factor two results from the fact, that there can't
> be done much more improvement on Intel chips any more.
> 100MFLOP are one mult/add every second cpu cycle on a 200MHz
> machine.

Improvement is possible, at least on Alphas. As a general rule
try to avoid superfluous array accesses, use more local variables
instead (which can more easily be mapped to registers by the
compiler).

I played around with your code and got a version which achives
324.00 MFLOPS now (compared to 187.86 MFLOPS with your code).
Note that in my version memcpy () takes 22.5 percent of total
computying time on my Alpha, so that your MFLOPS numbers are
too low as run time is measured overall.

I is now your part
 - to verify that my version gives right results
 - to let me know how my version performes on the PPro (especially
   gcc and egcs compiled)

[Robert: could you please compile my code on your Alpha using
         egcs and report your results to me]

Stefan Schroepfer
scr@iis.fhg.de

-----------------

#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <time.h>
#include <math.h>

#define N 1024

void trafo(double *a,const int n,double *wksp,double *const ops)
{
 double	c0 = 3.52262918857095333469e-02;
 double	c1 = -8.54412738820266443041e-02;
 double	c2 = -1.35011020010254584323e-01;
 double	c3 = 4.59877502118491543470e-01;
 double	c4 = 8.06891509311092547385e-01;
 double	c5 = 3.32670552950082631938e-01;
 int i,j;
 double *wksph=wksp+n/2;
 double	lfT1, lfT2;
 double	lfA0, lfA1;

 if(n<6)
    return;

 for(i=j=0;i<n/2-2;i++,j+=2)
 {
   /* 324.00 MFLOPS (reduced a[] - accesses) */
   lfA0 = a[j];		lfA1 = a[j+1];
   lfT1 =  c0*lfA0 + c1*lfA1;
   lfT2 =  c5*lfA0 - c4*lfA1;
   lfA0 = a[j+2];	lfA1 = a[j+3];
   lfT1 += c2*lfA0 + c3*lfA1;
   lfT2 += c3*lfA0 - c2*lfA1;
   lfA0 = a[j+4];	lfA1 = a[j+5];
   lfT1 += c4*lfA0 + c5*lfA1;
   lfT2 += c1*lfA0 - c0*lfA1;

   wksph[i] = lfT1;
   wksp[i]  = lfT2;

   /* 191.28 MFLOPS (cx variables instead of array c[]) */
   /*
   wksph[i] = (c0*a[j]   + c1*a[j+1] +
	       c2*a[j+2] + c3*a[j+3] +
	       c4*a[j+4] + c5*a[j+5]);
   wksp[i]  = (c5*a[j]   - c4*a[j+1] +
	       c3*a[j+2] - c2*a[j+3] +
	       c1*a[j+4] - c0*a[j+5]);
	       */
 }
 wksph[i] = (c0*a[j]   + c1*a[j+1] +
	     c2*a[j+2] + c3*a[j+3] +
	     c4*a[0]   + c5*a[1]);
 wksp[i]  = (c5*a[j]   - c4*a[j+1] +
	     c3*a[j+2] - c2*a[j+3] +
	     c1*a[0]   - c0*a[1]);
 i++;j+=2;
 wksph[i] = (c0*a[j] + c1*a[j+1] +
	     c2*a[0] + c3*a[1] +
	     c4*a[2] + c5*a[3]);
 wksp[i]  = (c5*a[j] - c4*a[j+1] +
	     c3*a[0] - c2*a[1] +
	     c1*a[2] - c0*a[3]);

 memcpy(a,wksp,sizeof(double)*n);
 (*ops)+=((double)11)*n;
 return;
}

int main(int argc,const char *argv[])
{
 double *x,h,ops=0;
 int i;
 clock_t start;

 if(!(x=malloc(2*N*sizeof(double))))
    {
     fputs("out of memory\n",stderr);
     return EXIT_FAILURE;
    }

 for(i=0;i<N;i++)
    {
     h=(double)i;
     x[i]=sin(h*M_PI/(double)N);
    }

 start=clock();
 for(i=0;i<10000;i++)
     trafo(x,N,x+N,&ops);
 h=(double)(clock()-start)/(double)CLOCKS_PER_SEC;
 printf("%.2f MFLOPS\n",1.0e-6*ops/h);

 free(x);
 return 0;
}


