On Wed, 24 Jun 1998, Christopher C Chimelis wrote:> 
> On Wed, 24 Jun 1998, Jeff Sturm wrote:
> > I got around 2.7 integer and 2.6 fp.  My 533MHz 164SX did 7.8 on integer
> > and died with a SIGFPE on fp (why?).  Running NT it completed with ~6.8 on
> 
> Try recompiling the Alpha version with the -mieee flag.  Chances are, you
> have a condition (ie div by zero) that triggers a floating point
> exception.  That flag should force egcs to include FPE handling code and
> will (or should) allow it to run without dying with an FPE.

I'm aware of -mieee and have used it for many applications that would 
otherwise die on SIGFPE.  But it is hardly ideal--it seems to slow down 
the benchmark code by a ratio of 3-4.  I'm not exactly sure what it does, 
but it seems to add a lot of fp value-checking code.  I can do much the 
same by inserting statements in my source code, all at the expense of 
runtime performance.

My real question is why do these programs run on x86 w/o -mieee?  GCC on 
x86 just returns NaN or Inf.  Trapping errors in hardware sounds a lot 
more efficient to me.  I can write a signal handler, but it's hard to 
recover.  I'd rather not get the signal at all.

BTW I have no problem with MS Visual C++.  Code from gcc causes SIGFPE, 
both on Linux and OpenNT.  What is it doing differently?


Jeff


