Quoting Matt Kimmel (matt@javanet.com):
> > the 'milo/images' directory (the directory exists on the CD, the book says
> > to get the image from there), but there were images in the 'milo'
> > directory.
> > 
> > I chose the 'xl' image (I have an Alpha XL 233), and wrote it to disk:
> > 
> >   dd if=xl of=/dev/fd0

If it's not in the milo/image directory, then it's NOT AN IMAGE!  It's a dos
file, and you copy it using dos tools.  (mcopy under linux, etc).  The "image"
files have the entire dos file system with the dos files already there, and
you can dd it onto a floppy.

> 1. Get a 3.5" disk formatted by DOS 6 or earlier (for some reason, ARC
> doesn't like FAT floppies formatted by Windows 95/DOS 7.  Dunno about the
> Linux MS-DOS tools.

You can either use mformat, or a combination of fdformat and mkfs.msdos.

> 2. Copy /images/arc/linload.exe to \linload.exe on the floppy.

Either mount the floppy (mount -t msdos) and use "cp", or use mcopy.

> 3. Copy the milo image from /milo (in your case /milo/xl) to the floppy as
> \milo.

Ditto.  If you mounted the floppy, be sure and umount it after you've finished
copying to make sure the buffers get flushed.

> 4. Follow the instructions in the RH5.1 Alpha Addendum.

"Follow the instructions".  Now *there's* a novel concept.


