On Fri, 24 Jul 1998, Keith Baker wrote:

> Ok...  I have both of the last numbers as 0's...  Fsck seems to insist
> that it be checked anyhow!  What do I need to do?  I do not have

 Just `ln -s ../bin/true /sbin/fsck.nfs', as I suggested in the other
mail.

> "hard,intr,nodev" in fstab instead I think it is what ever the
> standard fstab would have for /...  What exactly do these parameters do?

 You do not want to have "nodev" for root fs.  Unless you have your '/dev'
separate, which I doubt.  "Nodev" forbids device nodes operation.

 "Intr" and "hard" are related to NFS timeout treatment and are described
in 'nfs(5)' man page.

> Which parts of the filesystem can be shared accross many remote machines?
> I have read that /usr and /lib were safe (I think)...

 Basically you should avoid sharing of '/dev', '/etc', '/tmp' and '/var'.
Though with some effort it might be possible to share some or all of them.

> Also...  What shutdown order whould the machine have?  By default redhat
> blows up because it shuts off nfs and networking...  How can I shut down
> the system when the steps involve shutting down the filesystem which tell
> it how to continue shutting down?!?!

 Just deactivate 'K95nfsfs' symlink for runlevels 0 and 6.

