Markus Dickebohm writes:
> 
> I tried to compile the latest gnuplot beta version on our Alphastation 
> yesterday

Did you use -Wall and paid a close attention to what your compiler has
to say?  BTW - a version using 340 runs on various Alphas like a
clockwork.  I did not try so far with later versions.
 
> 
> Otherwise, you told GDB there was a function where there isn't one, or
> (more likely) you have encountered a bug in GDB.
> 0x1555610708c in ?? ()
> (gdb)                                                  
> 
> The program was compiled with "-g".
> Do I have a buggy gdb or is this a known bug in RH5.0?

Most likely you smashed a stack by passing to a function arguments of
sizes other that this function expected (missing prototypes?); or you
wrote over stack pieces - once again, because of sizes of some objects
were quietly assumed to be 4.  Once you did that your debugger is
understandably confused and tracking such errors with gdb is usually a
prolonged investigation.  It is much easier to catch them in an earlier
stage.

gdb does seem to have some problems of its own but in most cases
I have seen this was a programmer error.  Bugs in questions are
masked on 32-bit platforms, so they are quite popular, but they
are still ordinary bugs.

  Michal


