Hi,


I have a Cable Modem with a Pentium 233 running as a IP-Masquerading 
router to the internet and have experimented with direct access to 
internet and via the IP-Masq routing.  I have see NO (repeat NO) slowdown 
by using the routing.  This did not surprise me on a Pentium 233, but I 
have a friend who has a 486/33 running linux on 8Mb RAM and a 500 Mb HD 
with a pair of 3Com 3C509b cards and he sees NO slowdown either.  This 
did surprise me, but I confirmed it with a direct connection to a Pentium 
II 333 starting a download at 385 Kb per second and finishing at 500+ Kb 
per second.  Then I connected back up to the 486 and used IP-Masq and 
started my connection at 377 Kb per second and again ended with 500+ Kb 
per second throughput.  This impressed me greatly and showed that you 
don't need bleeding edge technology to route IP Packets, even with 
IP-Masq to slow it down a touch.  I so suggest you stick with good fast 
ethernet cards though.  I had no luck trying to NE2000 clones to work in 
this box. (actually one would work fine, but as paired with any other 
card, it wouldn't work)



