> directory but I keep getting the following error message:
> bash: ./rc5des: No such file or directory .
>
Do a "file rc5des". I'm interested in learning that this indeed is an
Alpha binary, 64-bit ELF.

Now, do an "ldd rc5des". This will give you a listing of shared libraries
referenced by the program, and where those resolve to on your system. E.g.,

        libc.so.6 => /lib/libc.so.6 (0x40006000)
        /lib/ld-linux.so.2 => /lib/ld-linux.so.2 (0x00000000)

If you don't see a library listed after the "=>", you're in trouble.

So, either the binary type is something the machine does not accept (do we
still support ECOFF on 5.1?) [e.g., is this a DEC UNIX or worse, an Intel 386
binary?], or one of the component shared libaries is missing.

Either cases triggers an error; bash assumes such errors occur due to the fact
that the file's not found. Not a stupid assumption; there's no further
information available to it.

