>> By trying to avoid reusing of the registers involved for some time and
>> moving the trap-barrier or eliminating it, the speed-penalty could 
>> probably be reduced for the most part.
> 
>>From what I understand the real problem is parallel instruction issue,
> not register usage.  The kernel cannot tell which instruction caused a 
> trap if -mieee is not used.  So -mieee must reduce floating-point to a 
> single CPU pipeline, there's the performance penalty.

Actually, it's not so much parallel instruction *issue* as it is
parallel instruction *execution*.  In other words, you can have several
instructions "in the pipeline" when a floating exception occurs.  The
current Alpha architecture does not provide for precise floating point
traps; depending on where in the pipeline the fault occurs, the PC
reported may be an arbitrary number of instructions *after* the PC of
the actual faulting instruction.

The trivial solution to this, of course, is as you described:  reduce
the effective "pipeline" length to one by issuing trapb's after every
floating-point instruction.  This provides a significant performance
penalty.

That's why the Alpha architecture provides the "register write mask"
and the register re-use rules, along with a fairly large number of
floating-point registers.  This way, a sophisticated compiler can
try to keep the pipeline full by issuing several floating-point
instructions before issuing a trapb...

