> Alpha offers several versions of fp-instructions. When you want full
> IEEE-compliance you have to choose the ones that trap on certain
> situations, so that the trap-handler can emulate it in software.
> 
> If you compile with gcc without -mieee it simply uses instructions that
> don't trap, AFAIK.

Not entirely true.  Some exceptions (e.g. underflow, inexact) can be
enabled or disabled on a per-instruction basis.  Others (e.g. overflow,
invalid operation) cannot; they're always enabled.

In fact, the vast majority of the problems that require -mieee involve
"invalid operation" traps.  These occur when you attempt to perform an
arithmetic operation involving a NaN ("Not a Number"), a denormal 
number, or an infinity.  The IEEE spec provides mechanisms whereby
these can be handled "quietly"; rather than signalling a program
exception, they can indicate the exception in their results.  So, for
example, if you do an "add" operation and one of the operands is a
NaN, the floating-point implementation can produce a NaN in the result
rather than interrupting program execution.

If an architecture (e.g. MIPS) provides precise floating-point traps,
then it's fairly cheap to trap the exception and either pass it up
as a SIGFPE or handle it quietly according to the IEEE spec.  The
imprecise traps on Alpha make this somewhat more costly.

Often, I find I have to resort to -mieee when porting sloppy code.
For example, a denorm is used where the programmer really meant to
use zero.  Since IEEE's quiet behavior is to treat the denorm as zero,
this code "works" on other architectures even if it's technically
incorrect.  Because it causes no failure, the programmer is not
inclined to fix it.

