On Mon, Jun 29, 1998 at 05:39:48PM -0400, Mike Tripicco wrote:
> The problem relates to some legacy Fortran code I've inherited which
> allows Fortran to do dynamic memory allocation. The trick involves Fortran
> handling the offset between the location of a common block set by the
> Fortran code and the location of the malloc'd memory. That offset is used
> by Fortran and thus must fit in a 4-byte integer. In this case that's
> impossible. 

Ah hah.  You can disable this ordinarily quite useful feature
of malloc by calling

	mallopt(M_MMAP_MAX, 0);

in your startup code.  See <malloc.h>.

> However, it looks to me like if the GNU linker had an option equivalent to
> Digital's "-taso" I'd be able to avoid the problem:

Yes, well, no one has had the incentive to work on that.  It 
would probably take about 2 months of work.


