> 
> But now I'm getting
> a 'missing module mt' message upon boot-up.  Strange, because I compiled
> in 'st' (SCSI tape) as part of the kernel.

As it was mentioned yesterday /etc/rc.d/rc.sysinit contains the
following code:


# If a SCSI tape has been detected, load the st module unconditionally
# since many SCSI tapes don't deal well with st being loaded and
# unloaded
if [ -f /proc/scsi/scsi ] &&\
 cat /proc/scsi/scsi | grep -q 'Type: Sequential-Access' 2>/dev/null ; then
        if cat /proc/devices | grep -qv ' 9 st' ; then
                if [ -n "$USEMODULES" ] ; then
                        # Try to load the module.  If it fails, ignore
                        # it...
                        modprobe st 2>/dev/null
                fi
        fi
fi

This error message you see **should** be redirected to /dev/null.
It is hard to tell what was modified in your startup files that you
see it on your screen.

One could, obviously, try to check if st.o exists in a required place
before calling modprobe, but no harm done is modprobe fails, so...

> I tried adding 'alias st off' in the /etc/conf.modules

It will be not consulted here as 'st' is loded explicitely.

> 
> Basically, I'm still in the Slackware mind-set (modify a /etc/rc.d/rc.modules
> file specifying which modules you want to load)

Ah, this file does not exist in a "normal" Red Hat distribution, so
maybe you attempt to load 'st' module twice?  You should be using
kerneld in most cases anyway or unused modules will be not automatically
unloaded (there are some changes in this mechanism in the current
development kernels, but the basic idea is still the same).

  Michal


